\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The coupled Hurwitz kernel has an alternating form}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Use the \vocabk{integral alternating Hurwitz form}{fdip-00V2}
on each based cochain slot and its direct-sum
\vocabk{cochain pairing}{fdip-00SR} #{B}. If there are #{N}
slots, the cochain module has rank #{4N}, and #{B} is
perfect alternating. For every link assignment and mass
#{m\in k}, put #{K_U=m\bbI +\extD_U}. Then
##{B(v,K_Uv)=0\quad\text{for every }v.}
Thus its \vocabk{kernel form}{fdip-00UV} is alternating, and
##{\det K_U=p_B(K_U)^2.}
In the basis ordered by slots and then #{(1,\qi,\qj,h)},
#{\pfaffian(B)=(-1)^N} and #{p_B(m\bbI)=m^{2N}}.
These are integral identities and remain valid in
characteristic #{2}.}

\proof{
\p{A direct sum of perfect alternating forms is perfect
alternating. Each block has Pfaffian #{-1}, so matching
within the blocks gives #{\pfaffian(B)=(-1)^N}.
By \ref{fdip-00V4}, coefficient transport has inverse-unit
adjoint. Reindexing the cochain sum and taking the signed
insertion/deletion adjoints, exactly as in \ref{fdip-00SS},
gives #{\extd_U^\dagger=\extdag_U} and
#{\extD_U^\dagger=\extD_U}.}

\p{Write #{v=\sum_pv_p} by cell degree. Distinct degrees
are orthogonal for #{B}. Since #{\extd_U} raises degree
and #{\extdag_U} lowers it,
##{\begin{aligned}
 B(v,\extD_Uv)
 &=\sum_p\bigl(B(v_{p+1},\extd_Uv_p)
                 +B(v_p,\extdag_Uv_{p+1})\bigr)\\
 &=\sum_p\bigl(B(v_{p+1},\extd_Uv_p)
                 +B(\extd_Uv_p,v_{p+1})\bigr)=0.
\end{aligned}}
Alternation also gives #{B(v,mv)=0}. This proves the
zero-diagonal condition directly, with no cancellation of
#{2}. The transpose identity
#{K_U^{\mathsf T}B=BK_U} follows from self-adjointness,
but the degree argument supplies the additional alternation
required by \ref{fdip-00UX}. That theorem gives the square
identity and the scalar value.}

\p{The same computation works first over #{\mathbb Z[m]}
and then under every scalar extension. Over #{\mathbb Q}
or #{\mathbb R}, the rational positive norm pairing has
exactly the same inverse-transport and exterior-slot adjoints.
It therefore gives the same algebraic operator #{\extD_U},
not a different kernel. In particular the large-mass result
\ref{fdip-00U7} applies to this kernel through its positive
norm pairing, while the integral square identity uses #{B}.}
}
